# sel: substrings (first line containing it); '@X' = line starting with X plus its table rows. trig = verbatim quotes. rule/formula one line.
W={}
def w(p,n,sel,trig,rule,formula): W[(p,n)]=dict(sel=sel,trig=trig,rule=rule,formula=formula)
# ---------- Paper 1 ----------
w(1,3,['Singh would like to take advantage'],["under-leveraged balance sheet","current shareholders retain control"],"Under-leveraged + shareholders must keep control + replace equity by debt = dividend recapitalization (debt-financed dividend or buyback). Reorganization = court-supervised insolvency; LBO = an acquirer buys the firm.","Dividend recap: debt up, equity down, stakes not diluted, WACC down.")
w(1,4,['Division B generated','A wholesale segment','A consumer segment','Singh evaluates if spinning off'],["valued at 6.0x EV/EBITDA","contributed 20% to the division's EBITDA","change in shareholder value of greater than 5%"],"Sum of the parts: each segment's EBITDA × ITS OWN comparable multiple, then compare with the combined EV. Material = change above ±5%.","SOTP = 7.0 × 20% × 10.8 + 5.1 × 80% × 10.8 = 59.184; combined = 6.0 × 10.8 = 64.8; change = −8.7%.")
w(1,6,['On 1 January of the current year'],["vest 3 years after the grant date","expire 6 years after the grant date"],"Option expense is spread over the VESTING period, not the term to expiry. One year's expense = grant-date fair value × options ÷ vesting years.","4m × EUR1.8 ÷ 3 = EUR2.4m.")
w(1,8,['@Exhibit 2'],["Liquidity Coverage Ratio | 95% | 110%","Net Stable Funding Ratio | 95% | 90%"],"NSFR = available ÷ required stable funding: below 100% means required > available; a higher NSFR than last year = improvement. LCR below 100% = cannot cover the 30-day stress outflows (it is short, not 5% to spare).","NSFR 95% < 100% → required stable funding is higher than available.")
w(1,9,['Grap backtests the strategy','Rebalancing frequency','Formation of factor portfolios','Weighting of factor portfolios'],["Rebalancing frequency: monthly (simulated using rolling windows)","Weighting of factor portfolios: risk parity"],"Two layers (factor portfolios, then risk-parity weights from a covariance matrix) = TWO rolling-window iterations; windows overlap; the in-sample period is the previous 12 months (not 13).","Layer 1: factor ranks over 12 months. Layer 2: 10-year covariance matrix → equal-risk-contribution weights.")
w(1,10,['The TOP100 Index is updated','Method A only uses','Method B only uses','Method C only uses'],["composition has significantly changed","Method A only uses the companies that were in the TOP100 Index at any given point in time t."],"Survivorship bias is avoided with point-in-time data (members as they were at each date, including later casualties). Methods that start from today's members, or from names present throughout, keep only survivors.","Point-in-time universe = no survivorship bias.")
w(1,12,['Grap performs a complementary risk analysis'],["absolute return trading strategy","benchmarked against cash","better understanding of the downside risk"],"Absolute return vs a cash benchmark: relative VaR equals plain VaR (adds nothing); surplus at risk is for pension liabilities; downside of a market-neutral strategy = maximum drawdown (worst peak-to-trough).","Cash benchmark has zero volatility → relative VaR = VaR.")
w(1,15,['Kassam evaluates the stock of Altair'],["stable market-based multiple","does not want to make any adjustments to the reported financial statements","internally generated brands"],"No adjustments wanted + unreliable earnings and book value (internal brands, discretionary expenses) → P/S: sales are hard to manipulate and more stable than EPS. P/B misses internal intangibles; P/E is distorted by expense choices.","P/S: stable, less manipulable, no adjustments needed.")
w(1,16,['Kassam values Becq','@Exhibit 3'],["excess earnings method","Required return on working capital | 3.25%","Discount rate | 11.25%"],"Excess earnings: residual = earnings − required returns on working capital and fixed assets; capitalise it with the DISCOUNT rate − g (not the asset return) to value the intangibles, then ADD the tangible assets for the whole business.","119,427.5 × 1.025 ÷ (11.25% − 2.5%) = 1,399,008; + 315,000 + 725,000 = 2.44m.")
w(1,17,['She first considers','Bond A is a 5%','To analyze these bonds','@Exhibit 1 Binomial'],["Bond A is a 5% annual pay coupon bond and is callable at par","interest rate volatility of 20%"],"Callable bond on the tree: work backwards from the end; at any node where the value exceeds the call price (100) the issuer calls, so cap the value at 100; then average the two nodes and discount with the node rate.","Year 1: 105/1.0609 = 98.97 and min(105/1.0408, 100) = 100; (100+5)/1.02 and (98.97+5)/1.02 → 102.44.")
w(1,19,['Plessinger next evaluates'],["callable investment-grade corporate bond","interest rate volatility increases from 20% to 25%","the bond's price is unchanged"],"Callable bond, price fixed, volatility up: the call option is worth more; Z-spread = OAS + option cost, so the OAS FALLS (for a putable bond it would rise).","Z-spread = OAS + option cost.")
w(1,20,['Finally, still using the binomial','@Exhibit 1 Binomial'],["capped floating rate bond","capped at 5.0%","set in arrears"],"A cap limits the coupon on paths where the tree rate exceeds the cap (6.09% in Year 1, 7.32% in Year 2), so the capped floater is worth LESS than par; without a cap a floater trades at par on reset dates.","Cap binds on some nodes → value < par.")
w(1,22,['Chang is also evaluating hedge funds','Strategy 1: Multi-strategy','Strategy 2: Fund of funds'],["Strategy 1: Multi-strategy funds","Strategy 2: Fund of funds","returns, leverage, and liquidity"],"Multi-strategy vs fund of funds: multi-strategy = higher returns, MORE leverage, more variance and more gates; funds of funds = more diversified mix, lower leverage, somewhat lower variance.","Multi-strategy: return up, leverage up, gates yes.")
w(1,24,['The pension plan has an objective'],["limit drawdowns","combined with the pension plan's stock and bond portfolio"],"Smallest maximum drawdown when added to a stock and bond portfolio: opportunistic strategies (global macro, systematic futures, merger arbitrage, equity market-neutral). Event-driven, relative value and long/short equity show high drawdowns.","Low drawdown contribution → global macro.")
w(1,25,['Moreno is tasked with a new project'],["not linearly separable","addresses non-linear separability and is not prone to overfitting"],"Non-linear separability + no overfitting → SVM with soft margin classification (penalty for misclassified points, wider margin). Non-linear SVM and unconstrained CART overfit.","Soft margin: trade-off wider margin vs total error penalty.")
w(1,26,['Moreno also creates Model 1','@Exhibit 1'],["PROFIT = –2.0 and LEVER = 3.0","Intercept | –1.7878"],"Logit: P = 1 ÷ (1 + exp(−(b0 + b1x1 + b2x2))). exp(linear part) alone is the ODDS, not the probability, and dropping the intercept gives 77%.","x = −1.7878 + 0.0370 + 1.1628 = −0.588; P = 1 ÷ (1 + e^0.588) = 0.357.")
w(1,27,['To analyze the drivers of ROE','@Exhibit 2'],["adding a third factor, the quick ratio (QUICK)","QUICK | 0.0919 | 1.9204 | 0.0558"],"Adding a variable never lowers R²; adjusted R² rises if the new coefficient's |t| > 1.0 (QUICK: 1.92), even though p = 0.0558 is not significant at 5%.","|t| > 1 → adjusted R² up; |t| < 1 → down.")
w(1,28,['Finally, Moreno wants to test','A plot of the regression residuals','Note: Exhibit 3'],["A plot of the regression residuals against SALES","the variance of the residuals increases as SALES increase"],"Residual variance that grows with the regressor = CONDITIONAL heteroskedasticity: biased standard errors, unreliable F-test, more Type I errors. Unconditional heteroskedasticity is not a problem. A residual plot does not show non-linearity of the relationship.","Fan-shaped residuals → conditional heteroskedasticity.")
w(1,31,['McCormick reviews his FCFE valuation model'],["will issue new common stock during the next year","next year's FCFE forecast"],"Share issuance, repurchases and dividends are USES of FCFE, not drivers of it: next year's FCFE is unchanged. FCFE changes only with CFO, capex and net borrowing.","FCFE = CFO − FCInv + net borrowing.")
w(1,35,['Chu reviews a diversified US bond portfolio','Sector A:','Sector B:','Sector C:'],["spreads can be expected to narrow the most","economy recovers from a business downturn"],"Spreads move most for low-rated AND cyclical issuers: they widen most in a downturn and narrow most in a recovery → speculative grade + cyclical = Sector B.","Low rating + cyclical = biggest spread swings.")
w(1,38,['Silva conducts due diligence'],["buys its stock for one of the equity funds he manages","Silva sells some of his personal position","as part of rebalancing his personal portfolio"],"VI(B) Priority of Transactions: client trades come first; personal trades are allowed if clients are not disadvantaged (the fund bought first, the stock is actively traded). A profit, or trading against his own recommendation, is not a violation by itself.","No client disadvantage → no violation.")
w(1,40,["Tritium's recommended policy"],["hard copy form","He saves his records for 5 years","Local regulations require record retention for 5 years"],"V(C): records may be kept in hard copy or electronic form, for the period required by regulation or firm policy; the 7-year recommendation applies only when neither exists.","Format free; period = regulation (5 years) ≥ OK.")
w(1,44,["Murphy's supervisor",'Policy 1','Policy 2'],["A monetary incentive will be offered as a reward for ethical behavior.","The deputy manager will be responsible for evaluating the ethical conduct of department members."],"IV(C): a supervisor of a large department may delegate to trained deputies and may reward ethical behavior, even with money; responsibility remains with the supervisor.","Incentive for ethics + trained delegate = compliant.")
# ---------- Paper 2 ----------
w(2,1,['Tay collects components','@Exhibit 1'],["Volume Growth | Price Growth | Foreign Currency Exchange","highest sustainable sales growth rate"],"Sustainable sales growth = volume + price; the foreign-exchange part is not sustainable.","Malaysia 4 + 5 = 9%; Thailand 5 + 0 = 5%; Hong Kong −4 + 8 = 4%.")
w(2,3,['Creamy Meadows is a subsidiary','@Exhibit 2'],["Buttery uses the temporal method","Weighted average rate when inventory was purchased | 14,700","Average for the most recent year | 14,700"],"Temporal method: monetary items at the current rate, inventory/COGS at historical rates, sales at the average rate. A ratio is unchanged only if numerator and denominator are translated at the same rate (COGS ÷ sales: 14,700 for both).","COGS (14,700) ÷ sales (14,700): same rate.")
w(2,5,['Danny Aoun is an equity portfolio manager','@Exhibit 1'],["more concerned about the models producing false negatives","Precision | Recall | F1 Score | Accuracy"],"False negatives costly → highest RECALL (TP ÷ (TP + FN)); false positives costly → precision; equal weight → accuracy or F1.","Recall: X 0.94 > Y 0.90 > Z 0.82.")
w(2,6,['For Bytewave, Aoun estimates'],["autoregressive conditional heteroskedasticity","ARCH(1) errors"],"ARCH(1): the error variance depends on the previous period's squared error, so it is NOT constant but predictable.","Var(εt+1) = a0 + a1·εt².")
w(2,10,['Team Green is asked','Point 1','Point 2','Point 3'],["Total factor productivity reflects the state of technology","the impact of investment spending on available capital depends on the existing physical capital stock"],"Labor productivity growth = capital deepening + TFP growth, so TFP is NOT 'capital deepening plus labor productivity'. The R&D–growth link is not clear-cut in the short run. Investment matters most where the capital stock is small.","Labor productivity growth = capital deepening + TFP growth.")
w(2,11,['Team Purple is asked'],["externalities associated with human capital","most important determinant in predicting the occurrence of convergence"],"Human capital and knowledge externalities = endogenous growth: no diminishing returns, so rich countries keep their lead → NON-convergence. Diminishing returns (neoclassical) → conditional or absolute convergence.","Externalities → no convergence.")
w(2,12,['Smith presents the expected impact','Description 1','Description 2','Description 3'],["opening up the economy should increase the rate at which countries' capital-to-labor ratios converge","Description 3: The rate of return on investments will be lower in countries with high capital-to-labor ratios."],"Opening up: capital flows to the low capital-to-labor country (high MPK). The exporter grows BELOW its steady state and runs trade SURPLUSES; the importer grows above steady state and runs deficits. Returns are lower where capital per worker is high.","High K/L → low MPK → capital leaves.")
w(2,15,['Li uses a two-factor model','@Exhibit 3'],["surprises in inflation, FINFL, and GDP growth, FGDP"],"Factors that are SURPRISES in macro variables = macroeconomic factor model; company attributes = fundamental; portfolios extracted from returns = statistical.","Macro: factor = surprise; fundamental: factor = attribute.")
w(2,16,['Li estimates the expected return','@Exhibit 3'],["expected return of the portfolio to be 1.8%","Actual Value (%) | Predicted Value (%) | Factor Sensitivity"],"R = a + Σ b × (actual − expected) + ε: use SURPRISES, and keep the intercept (expected return) and the error term.","1.8 + 3.0 × (1.2 − 0.8) + 0.8 × (−3.5 + 4.0) + 0.2 = 3.6%.")
w(2,18,['Using the spot curve, Li computes forward rates'],["in three years the 2-year spot rate will equal 2.20%","consistent with current forward rates"],"If the forecast future spot rate is LOWER than the implied forward rate, today's cash flows are discounted at too high a rate → the bond is undervalued (buy).","Forecast < forward → undervalued.")
w(2,20,['Li studies the characteristics'],["3.25% monthly standard deviation","higher key rate durations associated with longer-term rates leads to higher long-term annualized term structure volatility"],"Annualise volatility with × √12 (3.25% × 3.464 = 11.26%: correct). Key rate duration is the price sensitivity to ONE maturity's yield; it says nothing about how volatile rates are (short rates are usually more volatile than long rates).","σ annual = σ monthly × √12.")
w(2,21,['Option 1: Borrow','Option 2: Create','McAndrew considers the potential impact'],["will be able to influence the financial and operating policy of the SPE","expected to absorb any losses"],"Power over the SPE's policies + exposure to its losses = control (IFRS 10) → consolidate: the debt is on the consolidated balance sheet, the same as borrowing directly.","Control → consolidated ratio identical.")
w(2,25,['Firm A is a rapidly growing','The firm\'s dividend payout ratio','Planned expansion','Compared with peers'],["dividends exceeding free cash flow to equity","lower quality of accounting disclosure","regardless of future profitability"],"Pick the model from the facts: dividends not tied to profitability and above FCFE → free cash flow model; poor accounting quality rules out residual income; a DDM needs a payout policy tied to profits.","Dividends > FCFE → FCF model.")
w(2,26,['Firm B is a regional utilities','@Exhibit 1'],["within 2% of its estimated fair value","60% retention ratio"],"Payout = 1 − retention = 40% (not 60%): D = 40% × EPS; V0 = PV(dividends) + PV(year-3 price); within ±2% of fair value = fairly valued.","V0 = $18.70; 19.00 ÷ 18.70 − 1 = +1.6% → fairly valued.")
w(2,32,['The fund owns $5 million of a 7-year','Two months later','3-year subordinated','5-year senior unsecured bonds','7-year senior unsecured bonds trading'],["senior unsecured bond as its reference obligation","3-year subordinated unsecured bonds trading at 20% of par value","7-year senior unsecured bonds trading at 30% of par value"],"CDS payout = LGD of the cheapest-to-deliver bond of the SAME or higher seniority than the reference obligation (any maturity); a cheaper subordinated bond is not deliverable.","Cheapest senior = 30% → LGD 70% × $5m = $3.5m.")
w(2,38,['Friso compares two publicly traded'],["Perpetua Properties, a REIT","Rainfall Real Estate, a real estate operating company (REOC)"],"REIT: exempt from corporate tax if it distributes about 90–100% of taxable income → high payout, little retention; REOC: taxable, can retain earnings for growth.","REIT payout high; REOC flexible.")
w(2,41,['During their initial meeting'],["Dines knows that Prism's preferred broker would provide better execution","without further discussion she agrees to follow Kagan's wishes"],"III(A) directed brokerage: following the client's chosen broker is allowed, but you must TELL him it may cost best execution. A disclosed cost-benefit proxy policy is fine.","Directed broker → disclose possible loss of best execution.")
w(2,42,['At a subsequent meeting'],["Prism publishes a report changing its recommendation on Cromwell Inc., a cybersecurity company, to a sell","without informing him of the firm's recent recommendation change"],"III(B) Fair dealing: if a client orders against a current recommendation, tell him about the change before accepting the order. Paid premium calls after publication are fine (fair, not equal).","Order against recommendation → inform first.")
w(2,43,['Several months later'],["the past five years of return data for the top and bottom deciles of all Prism accounts","shown before taxes and gross of fees"],"III(D): present a weighted composite of similar portfolios, not selected deciles or accounts; disclosing 'gross of fees, before taxes' is adequate.","Composite = fair, accurate, complete.")
